// src/pipe_wire.pdx — mount.pdxfs.M3-003 (issue #9) — semantic-pipe
// PdxFsMountRecord@0.1 schema bind + emit, falling back to a documented
// line-based rendering.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why this is a fallback, not a real libpdx-semantic-pipe bind
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `libpdx-semantic-pipe` (github.com/paideia-os/libpdx-semantic-pipe) is
// a real, released library (v1.0.0, wave R49, M1-M5 landed) with a
// working `Send::send_record(fd, rec_ptr, rec_len)` entry point that
// frames a record as `[8B R20b header | 32B schema hash | rec_len body]`
// over `sys_ipc_send`. A caller binds a schema to an fd one of two ways:
// `Binding::libpdx_semantic_pipe_bind(fd, schema_hash_ptr)` (a raw
// 32-byte hash the caller already knows) or `Registry::bind_by_name(fd,
// name_ptr, name_len)` (resolve a schema NAME like "PdxFsMountRecord" to
// its hash via `svc.schema-registry`).
//
// `Registry::bind_by_name` is confirmed INERT as of this landing: per
// paideia-os#2000 ("R90-XREPO.006 Stand up libpdx-schema-registry
// service"), the service broker only knows two hardcoded test names
// (`svc.foo`/`svc.foobar`) and no `libpdx-schema-registry` service exists
// anywhere in the org -- `bind_by_name` always returns 304 (LOOKUP_FAIL).
// `mkfs.pdxfs`'s own `src/pipe_wire.pdx` (M3-003, #10) hit the identical
// wall over the identical schema-registry gap and reached the identical
// fallback conclusion; this file mirrors that landing's shape exactly,
// swapped to this tool's own schema name and record shape.
//
// Per this issue's own fallback instruction, `mount.pdxfs` therefore
// does NOT link `libpdx-semantic-pipe` at M3: every PdxFsMountRecord@0.1
// this tool emits is the SAME line-based `sys_write(1, ...)` rendering
// M1/M2's `MountRecord::mount_record_emit_result` already produces (src/
// mount_record.pdx), now preceded by one documented header line
// (`mount_pipe_lit_header` below) that tells a human or script reading stdout
// exactly why: "semantic-pipe emit deferred (svc.schema-registry not yet
// live)". The field values themselves are unaffected. Once
// `libpdx-schema-registry` lands and #2000 closes, this file's one
// wrapper is the ONLY call site that needs to change (to a real `bind_
// by_name` + `send_record` pair) -- `main.pdx` already goes through it
// rather than calling `MountRecord::mount_record_emit_result` directly,
// so this is a pure body swap with no ripple.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// One wrapper, not two — a deliberate simplification over mkfs.pdxfs's
// own M3-003 shape
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// `mkfs.pdxfs`'s own `pipe_wire.pdx` carries TWO wrappers
// (`mkfs_sp_emit_dry_run` / `mkfs_sp_emit_result`) because that repo's
// `FormatRecord` module still has two independent emitters with
// different argument shapes (a 3-argument dry-run preview and a
// separate 3-argument result emitter) left over from its own M1/M2
// history. `mount.pdxfs`'s own M2 landing already UNIFIED this: `src/
// mount_record.pdx`'s `mount_record_emit_result` is the single emitter
// every M2 code path calls, dry-run included (`MR_RESULT_DRY_RUN` is
// just one more `result_code` value among four, not a separate function
// signature) -- `mount_record_emit_dry_run` (the older M1 shape) still
// exists but has had no M2/M3 caller since M2 landed. There is therefore
// exactly ONE record-shaped call site left to wrap here:
// `mount_pipe_emit_result`, a thin 5-argument passthrough to `mount_
// record_emit_result` with the deferral header printed first. `main.pdx`
// calls this one wrapper on every terminal path (dry-run, elevation-
// stub, every failure-taxonomy code, and real success alike).
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Why the header is printed before every record rather than once
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Every code path in this repo emits at most one terminal
// PdxFsMountRecord@0.1 per process invocation. Printing the header
// unconditionally immediately before that single record is therefore
// already equivalent to "print once per process" in every reachable
// call graph this repo has; adding a "have I already printed this"
// latch would be defensive complexity with no reachable case where it
// changes output -- identical reasoning to mkfs.pdxfs's own pipe_wire.pdx.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Register-preservation note: why the incoming arguments are staged
// into callee-save registers, not caller-save scratch
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// This wrapper issues one `sys_write` (for the header) BEFORE
// delegating to `mount_record_emit_result`, which needs all five
// original incoming arguments. Per this repo's own established
// convention (mirroring mkfs.pdxfs's design/architecture.md §7,
// restated in its own `format_record.pdx`): the kernel's #743 info-leak
// hardening zeroes every CALLER-save register (`rdi`/`rsi`/`rdx`/`r8`/
// `r9`/`r10`) -- not just the usual `rcx`/`r11` SYSCALL clobbers --
// before every `sysret`. Staging the incoming arguments into any
// caller-save register across the header's `syscall` instruction would
// therefore silently lose them. This function instead stages all FIVE
// incoming arguments into the five SysV callee-save registers this
// codebase reserves for exactly this purpose (`rbx`/`r12`/`r13`/`r14`/
// `r15`), pushed on entry and popped (reverse order) before the single
// `ret` -- one more register than mkfs.pdxfs's own two 3-argument
// wrappers need, since `mount_record_emit_result`'s own 5-argument
// signature (volume_cap_ptr, mount_point_ptr, flags, result_code,
// mount_id) is wider than either of `FormatRecord`'s two emitters.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
// Encoder-gap discipline
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// No `add reg, [mem]` anywhere. No `test` mnemonic. Reserved-label
// discipline: this file's one function needs no internal branch labels.
//
// Upstream: design/tooling/r49-r50-plan.md §3.2 (paideia-os, the
// semantic-pipe design); github.com/paideia-os/libpdx-semantic-pipe (the
// real, released library this file does not yet link); GitHub issue
// paideia-os#2000 (the inert-registry blocker this file documents and
// works around); mkfs.pdxfs's own src/pipe_wire.pdx (M3-003, #10 -- the
// sibling-repo precedent this file's fallback posture and write-up
// structure mirror).

module PipeWire = structure {

  // --- Deferral header, printed to fd 1 immediately before every
  //     PdxFsMountRecord@0.1 line this repo emits. Length verified via
  //     `python3 -c "print(len(...))"`. ------------------------------------
  pub let mount_pipe_lit_header     : [u8; 133] = "# semantic-pipe emit deferred (svc.schema-registry not yet live, paideia-os#2000) -- PdxFsMountRecord@0.1 line-based fallback below\n\0"
  pub let mount_pipe_lit_header_len : u64 = 132

  // ==========================================================================
  // mount_pipe_emit_result(volume_cap_ptr, mount_point_ptr, flags,
  //                       result_code, mount_id) -> ()
  //
  // Thin wrapper around MountRecord::mount_record_emit_result (M2, src/
  // mount_record.pdx, extended at M3 #10 with the full failure-taxonomy
  // result codes -- see that file's own header): prints mount_pipe_lit_header
  // first, then delegates all five arguments unchanged. This is now
  // main.pdx's ONLY call site for a terminal PdxFsMountRecord@0.1 emit --
  // no code path in this repo calls MountRecord::mount_record_emit_
  // result directly any more.
  //
  // Register plan: rbx=volume_cap_ptr, r12=mount_point_ptr, r13=flags,
  // r14=result_code, r15=mount_id, all SysV callee-save, pushed on entry
  // and popped before the single `ret` -- see module header's note on
  // why a caller-save staging register would not survive the
  // intervening header `syscall`.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let mount_pipe_emit_result : (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> () !{mem, sysreg} @{fs} =
    fn (volume_cap_ptr: u64) (mount_point_ptr: u64) (flags: u64) (result_code: u64) (mount_id: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {mem, sysreg}, capabilities: {fs},
      justification: "mount.pdxfs.M3-003 (#9): mount_pipe_emit_result entry point. push rbx; push r12; push r13; push r14; push r15 before repurposing any. rbx=volume_cap_ptr, r12=mount_point_ptr, r13=flags, r14=result_code, r15=mount_id. sys_write(1, mount_pipe_lit_header, 132) -- SC+ ID 1, rdi/rsi/rdx/rax all freshly loaded for this call (per module header, none of rbx/r12/r13/r14/r15 are touched by it or by the kernel's post-syscall caller-save zeroing). Then rdi=rbx, rsi=r12, rdx=r13, rcx=r14, r8=r15, `call mount_record_emit_result` (src/mount_record.pdx) -- delegates the real rendering verbatim, now covering every MR_RESULT_* code as of M3 #10. Pops r15/r14/r13/r12/rbx (reverse push order) and returns (no value; caller sys_exits itself, matching mount_record_emit_result's own contract). No `add reg,[mem]`. No `test`. This function has no internal branch, so no label prefix is needed.",
      block: {
        push rbx;
        push r12;
        push r13;
        push r14;
        push r15;

        mov rbx, rdi;                       // rbx = volume_cap_ptr
        mov r12, rsi;                       // r12 = mount_point_ptr
        mov r13, rdx;                       // r13 = flags
        mov r14, rcx;                       // r14 = result_code
        mov r15, r8;                        // r15 = mount_id

        mov rdi, 1;
        lea rsi, [rip + mount_pipe_lit_header];
        mov rdx, 132;                       // mount_pipe_lit_header_len
        mov rax, 1;                         // SC+ ID 1 = sys_write
        syscall;

        mov rdi, rbx;
        mov rsi, r12;
        mov rdx, r13;
        mov rcx, r14;
        mov r8, r15;
        call mount_record_emit_result;

        pop r15;
        pop r14;
        pop r13;
        pop r12;
        pop rbx;
        ret;
      }
    }
}
